# Beer Stripe Tracker - Setup Guide

## What's included

- `beer-tracker/` - runs on the Chromebook (touchscreen device)
- `pc-receiver/` - runs on your main PC (backup storage)

## 1. Find each device's WireGuard IP

Since the Chromebook and PC are on different networks, use the
WireGuard tunnel IP for each device (not their regular WiFi/LAN IP) -
that's what lets them reach each other regardless of which network
either one is actually on.

On each device, run:
```bash
ip addr show wg0
```
(or check `wg show` if your interface isn't named `wg0`)

You're looking for the address WireGuard assigned to that tunnel,
typically something like `10.x.x.x`.

Make sure WireGuard is enabled to auto-connect on boot on both
devices (`sudo systemctl enable wg-quick@wg0`), otherwise the tunnel
might be down after a reboot and syncing will silently fail until
someone reconnects it manually.

## 2. Set up the PC receiver (do this first)

Your receiver PC is Linux Mint, so:

```bash
cd pc-receiver
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
python3 receiver.py
```

This starts a server on port 8000 (port 6000 is blocked by Chrome and
Firefox as an "unsafe port", so we use 8000 instead). Check it's
working by visiting `http://localhost:8000/totals` in a browser - you
should see `{}`.

Leave this running whenever you want backups to arrive. If your PC is
off or asleep, taps on the Chromebook still save locally and will
sync automatically once you turn the PC back on and the Chromebook
retries (every 30 seconds).

## 3. Configure the Chromebook app

Open `beer-tracker/app.py` and edit these lines near the top:

```python
PC_SYNC_URL = "http://<PC's WireGuard IP>:8000/receive"
HOUSEMATES = ["Sanne", "Tim", "Julia", "Bram", "Noor"]  # <- your actual housemates
ADMIN_PASSWORD = "changeme"  # <- pick a real password, keep it the same on the PC side
```

Also open `pc-receiver/receiver.py` and set:

```python
CHROMEBOOK_URL = "http://<Chromebook's WireGuard IP>:5000/api/restock"
ADMIN_PASSWORD = "changeme"  # <- must match the value in beer-tracker/app.py
```

## 4. Run the Chromebook app

```bash
cd beer-tracker
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
python3 app.py
```

Visit `http://localhost:5000` in a browser on the Chromebook. You
should see the button grid.

## 5. Make it start automatically in kiosk mode (Ubuntu)

Create a systemd service so the Flask app always runs:

```bash
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/beer-tracker.service
```

```ini
[Unit]
Description=Beer Stripe Tracker
After=network.target

[Service]
ExecStart=/home/<your-user>/beer-tracker/venv/bin/python3 /home/<your-user>/beer-tracker/app.py
WorkingDirectory=/home/<your-user>/beer-tracker
Restart=always
User=<your-user>

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

```bash
sudo systemctl enable beer-tracker
sudo systemctl start beer-tracker
```

Then autostart Chromium in kiosk mode pointed at the page. Create:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/.config/autostart
nano ~/.config/autostart/kiosk.desktop
```

```ini
[Desktop Entry]
Type=Application
Exec=chromium-browser --kiosk --incognito --noerrdialogs --disable-infobars http://localhost:5000
X-GNOME-Autostart-enabled=true
```

Also set the Chromebook to auto-login (Settings > Users) and disable
screen sleep (Settings > Power) so it stays on and awake as a wall
display.

## 6. Check backups on the PC anytime

Visit `http://localhost:8000/totals` on the PC, or open
`pc-receiver/beers_backup.db` with any SQLite browser
(e.g. `sqlite3 beers_backup.db "SELECT * FROM beers;"`).

## Notes

- Each tap is saved locally on the Chromebook first (`beers.db`), so
  nothing is lost even if the PC or WiFi is down.
- The `/api/resync` endpoint retries any unsent taps every 30 seconds
  automatically - no manual intervention needed.
- To add/remove housemates, just edit the `HOUSEMATES` list in `app.py`
  and restart the service.

## Stock panel & restocking

The side panel on the touchscreen shows:
- **Beschikbaar** (available) - bought amount minus what's been drunk since the last restock
- **Gedronken** (drunk) - beers tapped since the last restock
- **Tempo (24u)** (pace) - beers tapped in the last rolling 24 hours

**Restocking** (tap "Nieuwe voorraad" on the touchscreen, or use the PC
admin page) resets the available/drunk counters, sets a new bought
amount, and also resets each housemate's tap count back to 0 - so the
leaderboard, crown, and jester badge all restart fresh for the new
crate. The raw tap history itself isn't deleted (it's just filtered
by "since the last restock" everywhere it's displayed), so nothing is
actually lost if you ever want to look back at totals across restocks.

Restocking is password-protected (`ADMIN_PASSWORD` in both `app.py`
and `receiver.py` - make sure they match) since it's a destructive
admin action, not something any housemate should trigger by accident.

### Out-of-stock protection

Once `available` hits 0, the button grid is covered with a red
"Contacteer de Beheerder" screen and further taps are rejected by the
backend too (not just hidden in the UI), so the count can never go
negative even if two people tap at the exact same moment on the last
beer. The screen disappears on its own as soon as an admin restocks
(`available` goes back above 0).

### Screensaver

After 90 seconds of no touches, a full-screen "Du Pont Pilsch
Solutions" screensaver appears in an old-German blackletter style. A
single tap anywhere on it dismisses it immediately - that tap is only
used to close the screensaver, it doesn't also register as a beer.
To change how long it waits before appearing, edit `SCREENSAVER_IDLE_MS`
near the bottom of `static/script.js` (currently 90000ms = 90 seconds).

### PC admin dashboard

Visit `http://<pc-wireguard-ip>:8000/admin` in a browser on your
PC to see the same stock stats, the per-person totals, and a restock
form. Restocking from here forwards the action to the Chromebook (so
it stays the source of truth) and also logs it locally as a backup.
If the Chromebook is unreachable when you restock from the PC, you'll
get a warning that it's out of sync until it's back online.
